KW  - 1880–1900 and the 1891 letters
N1  - Salt Lake Tribune, May 31 to July 5, 1891. “Charity for the Mormons,” letters 1–6 of “The Elder”: May 31, p. 10 (“Charity for the Mormons”); June 7, p. 16 (“Church of God and Kingdom of God”); June 14, p. 17 (“The Apostles Ancient and Modern”); June 21, p. 3 (“The Priesthood Mistake”); June 28, p. 20 (“A Church That Is Fallen”); July 5, p. 3 (“Are the Mormons Sincere?”). The first six letters, on the Latter-day Saint church: the first is signed “an Elder of the Old-time Church, and a friend to the Mormon people,” and calls 1891 “a year of expectancy of great things.” The second argues that the kingdom of God is yet to come (the letter John R. Eardley described as Parson’s, on “the difference existing between a church and a kingdom”). The third calls Joseph Morris “a reforming prophet,” “one of the most innocent, meek, and inoffensive men that ever lived,” who went to Brigham Young and each of the apostles. The fourth teaches priesthood by birthright, to be shown by a prophet with the Urim and Thummim. The sixth urges that Joseph Smith III be invited to lead a united church and says the first Morrisites were cut off for refusing to testify that Brigham Young was a prophet. Other links: June 7: https://newspapers.lib.utah.edu/details?id=12468328; June 14: https://newspapers.lib.utah.edu/details?id=12469486; June 21: https://newspapers.lib.utah.edu/details?id=12470422; June 28: https://newspapers.lib.utah.edu/details?id=12471745; July 5: https://newspapers.lib.utah.edu/details?id=12472799 Listed on https://morrisite.org/newspapers.html#np-1891
