AU  - Kelley, Roger
TI  - From Morrisites to Marcus Daly
T2  - Montana Standard
CY  - Butte
PY  - 1989
DA  - 1989/07/02/
SP  - 69–71
KW  - Recent retellings, 1951 to the present
N1  - Montana Standard (Butte), July 2, 1989, pp. 69–71. Roger Kelley, “From Morrisites to Marcus Daly.” An interview with the Anaconda historian Alice Finnegan. She calls the Morrisites the area’s first settlers, says they wintered at Gregson (now Fairmont Hot Springs) before homesteading, and names Morgan Evans, who bought the land for Daly’s town and smelter, as a Morrisite. Morris’s Anaconda, Montana (1997) describes Evans’s arrival in 1866 without calling him one, and he is not on the 1861–62 roll; the claim is unconfirmed. Brought to this guide’s attention by Jim and Barbara Furshong. Listed on https://morrisite.org/newspapers.html#np-1951
ID  - montanastandard19890702
ER  - 

TY  - NEWS
TI  - Farmington Cannon Stays despite Claim
T2  - Standard-Examiner
CY  - Ogden
PY  - 1989
DA  - 1989/09/11/
SP  - 15
UR  - https://newspapers.lib.utah.edu/details?id=31321143
KW  - Recent retellings, 1951 to the present
N1  - Standard-Examiner (Ogden), September 11, 1989, p. 15. Valerie Phillips (Lakeside Review), “Farmington Cannon Stays despite Claim.” The Daughters of Utah Pioneers’ 1947 marker by the old cannon at Farmington says it was used at Nauvoo, brought west in 1847 and used in the “Morrisite battle at Uintah in 1862.” The cannon collector James H. Ambrose of Vernal identified it as a Civil War gun cast by the Joseph Reid Anderson company of Richmond, Virginia, in 1864, so it cannot have been at the siege. The Anderson family also took it for the gun that wounded Mary Christoffersen Anderson (Peter Anderson, Frontier Times, June–July 1965, p. 48). Listed on https://morrisite.org/newspapers.html#np-1951
ID  - standardexaminer19890911
